The idea that AI harms are discrete bugs fixed within two adaptive iterations, or that independent oversight is simply "friction," ignores the structural impacts already hitting workers and the economy. When Progressive uses AI to sort hundreds of thousands of job applications, as documented by "one-common-thread-some-companies-seeing-most-success-ai-2026-2," and this system systematically biases against certain demographics or skill sets, that's not a bug that gets patched. That's a systemic barrier to entry, a persistent exclusion that compounds over time, not a glitch. The real harm isn't a single inference error; it's the erosion of fair access to employment for millions, a condition that no mere software update will fix.

Accelerationist asks for specific deployments where things got measurably worse and weren't corrected. Look at the labor market. "Project Iceberg: The Iceberg Index: Measuring Skills-centered Exposure in the AI Economy" reported over 100,000 job losses linked to AI restructuring in 2025 alone. Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei forecast 10-20 percent unemployment for white-collar jobs within five years, a threat highlighted in "AI-Driven Worker Displacement Is a Serious Threat." These aren't temporary glitches. These are permanent displacements, entire career paths made obsolete, and a growing pool of workers without the adaptive capacity to retrain, particularly the 6.1 million clerical and administrative workers identified as vulnerable. This is not a "patchable" problem; it's a fundamental shift in economic power that requires massive, coordinated public investment and policy, not just corporate self-correction. Who pays for the lost wages, the retraining, the devastated communities? Right now, those costs are externalized onto the public.

The "liberation from scarcity" narrative sounds good on a TED Talk stage, but on the ground, it translates to wealth concentration and intensified labor for those left behind. While AI promises to free us, the reality is a 23-year-old in Nairobi labeling violent content for $2 an hour, ensuring your AI passes its safety audit. That's not liberation; that's the same old exploitative labor under a new tech veneer, and it's certainly not getting "fixed" in two iterations of a software update. Independent regulatory bodies are not just "friction"; they are the only mechanism that can demand accountability for these externalized costs and ensure that the immense profits generated by AI are not privatized while its social burdens are socialized. Without them, we just get more of the same: incumbents pulling up the drawbridge, protecting their market share, and leaving everyone else to adapt or perish.

How does "adaptive safety integration" address the erosion of human capital when women's jobs are three times more likely to be automated by AI than men's, as reported in "Women are avoiding the very technology that threatens them most, as expert warns of a â€˜two-tiered AI economyâ€™ approaching"? How does it account for the systemic bias embedded in algorithms that decide who gets a job, a loan, or even medical care, when those biases are not simple bugs but reflections of historical data and human design choices? These are not discrete harms at "the point of inference" that can be patched in real-time. These are structural inequities, baked into the very foundation of AI deployment. Who, exactly, is going to make these companies pay for the damage they cause, and what enforcement mechanism will ensure that "adaptive iteration" doesn't just mean "we'll fix it after we break it, maybe"?

